.TH "dzen2" "1" "" ""
.SH NAME
\fBdzen2\fP \- scriptable X11 status bars,
notifications and menus
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
\fBdzen2\fP [OPTIONS] < input
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Hello\[aq] | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP
status.sh | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-w\fP 800 \fB\-ta\fP l
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Menu\[aq] \[aq]xterm\[aq] \[aq]xclock\[aq] | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-m\fP v \fB\-p\fP
.EE
.PP
Use \fBdzen2\-help\fP to read this document
with live formatting.
Right\-click or press Escape to close that viewer.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Dzen displays a stream of newline\-terminated text from standard input.
Your script supplies data and determines when updates happen; dzen draws
it and handles mouse, keyboard and signal events.
It runs on X11.
.PP
Use it for a status bar, a notification, a scrollable log or a menu.
Text can contain colors, fonts, icons, shapes and clickable regions.
Optional build features include XFT, XPM, Xinerama, XRandR and Xcursor.
The gadgets directory supplies dbar, gdbar, gcpubar and textwidth
helpers.
.SH FORK DIFFERENCES
Comparison baseline: the upstream robm/dzen history at commit
488ab66019f475e35e067646621827c18a879ba1 (2013\-09\-23), before this
fork\[cq]s local development.
This is a historical comparison, not a claim about the latest upstream
version.
Fork repository: https://github.com/osv/dzen
.IP \[bu] 2
Resource reuse: color/font caches reduce repeated X11 resource
allocation.
.IP \[bu] 2
Input capacity: upstream\[cq]s 8192\-byte buffer is replaced by a
streaming reader accepting up to 16 MiB per line.
See LIMITATIONS.
.IP \[bu] 2
Layout: \fB\[ha]left()\fP,
\fB\[ha]center()\fP and
\fB\[ha]right()\fP provide independently
aligned regions in one instance.
They reset formatting for the following region.
.IP \[bu] 2
Runtime themes: \fB\[ha]normfg()\fP,
\fB\[ha]normbg()\fP and
\fB\[ha]normfn()\fP change normal defaults.
.IP \[bu] 2
Box model: \fB\-b\fP /
\fB\[ha]border()\fP and
\fB\-pad\fP /
\fB\[ha]padding()\fP add borders and
padding.
.IP \[bu] 2
Decorations: underline and overline spans cover text, icons and
graphics.
.IP \[bu] 2
Monitors: XRandR output selection follows changes and disconnection.
.IP \[bu] 2
Interaction: optional Xcursor feedback over clickable areas.
.IP \[bu] 2
Compatibility: \fB\-p\fP
\fIN\fP persistence takes pointer
presence into account.
hide fully unmaps hidden surfaces; upstream left a one\-pixel strip.
A hidden surface needs a signal or active key grab to restore it.
.IP \[bu] 2
Lifecycle: SIGTERM runs onexit and returns 143.
.IP \[bu] 2
Development: GNU Autotools builds and unit/integration tests.
.PP
Traditional stdin formatting, title/slave windows and event bindings
remain the basis of the interface.
Review INPUT PROTOCOL and EVENTS AND ACTIONS when adapting scripts,
especially custom \fB\-e\fP bindings and
whole\-line commands.
.SH QUICK START
Keep a message visible after its producer exits:
.IP
.EX
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Hello world\[aq] | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP
.EE
.PP
Without \fB\-l\fP, every new line replaces
the title:
.IP
.EX
while :; do
    date \[aq]+%H:%M:%S\[aq]
    sleep 1
done | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-w\fP 240 \fB\-ta\fP c
.EE
.PP
Show a title and two body lines immediately:
.IP
.EX
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Status\[aq] \[aq]Service running\[aq] \[aq]Connected\[aq] |
    \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-p\fP \fB\-e\fP \[aq]onstart=uncollapse;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Specifying \fB\-e\fP replaces ALL default
event bindings.
This example explicitly opens the slave window and binds right\-click to
exit.
.PP
Show a popup with the five processes using the most CPU (the popup
closes after 3 seconds):
.IP
.EX
{
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Top CPU processes\[aq]
    printf \[aq]%\-7s %6s  %s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]PID\[aq] \[aq]CPU %\[aq] \[aq]COMMAND\[aq]
    LC_ALL=C ps \-eo pid=,pcpu=,comm= \-\-sort=\-pcpu |
        head \-n 5
} | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP 3 \[rs]
    \fB\-l\fP 6 \fB\-ta\fP l \fB\-sa\fP l \fB\-x\fP \-50 \fB\-y\fP 50 \fB\-w\fP 420 \fB\-b\fP 1,grey70 \fB\-pad\fP 5,15 \[rs]
    \fB\-e\fP \[aq]onstart=uncollapse;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.SH INPUT PROTOCOL
The title is a single line.
The optional slave is a scrollable body.
In the usual vertical layout it opens below the title; placement near
the bottom of the screen can put it above the title instead.
.IP
.EX
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| Title: one line           |  first input line, or \fB\[ha]tw()\fPTEXT
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| Slave: body line 1        |  subsequent input lines
| Slave: body line 2        |  \fB\-l\fP \fIN\fP controls visible body lines
| ...                       |  wheel scrolls through the buffer
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.PP
Without \fB\-l\fP, only the title is used.
A collapsed slave retains its contents but is not visible.
Horizontal menu mode (\fB\-m\fP h) displays
slave entries in a single row and does not display title content.
.IP
.EX
producer \-> newline\-terminated stdin \-> title / slave
mouse, keyboard, signals \-> event bindings \-> actions / stdout / processes
.EE
.PP
Without \fB\-l\fP, each ordinary input line
replaces the title.
With \fB\-l\fP
\fIN\fP, the first ordinary line supplies
the title and subsequent lines enter the slave buffer; N is the number
of visible body lines, not the input buffer length.
By default the slave opens when the pointer enters the title.
.PP
There are three kinds of input:
.IP \[bu] 2
Rendered lines: text mixed with inline commands such as
\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIred\fP\fB)\fP.
Formatting starts from normal defaults for each line.
.IP \[bu] 2
Routed lines: \fB\[ha]tw()\fPTEXT
explicitly updates the title.
Put \fB\[ha]tw()\fP first, once per line.
It does not append TEXT to the slave.
.IP \[bu] 2
Control lines: \fB\[ha]cs()\fP,
\fB\[ha]padding(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
and the other CONTROL COMMANDS.
Each must occupy its own line.
They are consumed, not displayed as text.
.PP
Write a newline after each message.
Flush buffered output in your producer when an update should become
visible.
Double a caret to display it literally.
Input examples show the characters to send to dzen; README.dzen doubles
their carets again so they can be read in the live viewer.
.PP
Inline color/font changes affect subsequent content in that line.
\fB\[ha]fg()\fP,
\fB\[ha]bg()\fP and
\fB\[ha]fn()\fP restore normal defaults;
they do not mean \[lq]restore the previous span\[rq].
Alignment commands start a new region and reset formatting.
Runtime normal\-default commands affect subsequent drawing; resend
content when you want a full repaint with a new theme.
.PP
EOF normally ends dzen.
\fB\-p\fP keeps it alive;
\fB\-p\fP
\fIN\fP adds a timeout after EOF.
See EXIT STATUS for timeout and signal details.
.SH OPTIONS
Appearance:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-fg\fP \fICOLOR\fP             normal foreground
\fB\-bg\fP \fICOLOR\fP             normal background
\fB\-fn\fP \fIFONT\fP              normal font; xft: and x: select a backend
\fB\-fn\-preload\fP \fIFONT1,FONT2,...\fP  preload fonts for numbered aliases
\fB\-b\fP \fISPEC\fP               outer border widths and optional color
\fB\-pad\fP \fISPEC\fP             padding widths
\fB\-underline\fP \fIN[,COLOR]\fP  default underline thickness and optional color
\fB\-overline\fP \fIN[,COLOR]\fP   default overline thickness and optional color
\fB\-ta\fP \fIl|c|r\fP             title alignment (left, center or right)
\fB\-sa\fP \fIl|c|r\fP             slave alignment
.EE
.PP
Geometry and window properties:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-x\fP \fIPIXELS\fP             content x position
\fB\-y\fP \fIPIXELS\fP             content y position
\fB\-w\fP \fIPIXELS\fP             slave width (also the title fallback width)
\fB\-tw\fP \fIPIXELS\fP            explicit title width
\fB\-h\fP \fIPIXELS\fP             line height
\fB\-geometry\fP \fIWxH+X+Y\fP     title geometry in X geometry syntax
\fB\-expand\fP \fIleft|center|right\fP  grow/shrink title to its content
\fB\-dock\fP                 set dock type and reserve space through EWMH struts
\fB\-title\-name\fP \fINAME\fP      title/outer name (default: dzen title)
\fB\-slave\-name\fP \fINAME\fP      slave name (default: dzen slave)
.EE
.PP
Input and interaction:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-l\fP \fIN\fP                  visible slave lines; see MULTI\-LINE WINDOWS
\fB\-m\fP \fI[v|h]\fP              vertical (default) or horizontal menu; requires \fB\-l\fP
\fB\-e\fP \fIBINDINGS\fP           events and actions; replaces all default bindings
\fB\-p\fP \fI[SECONDS]\fP          persist after EOF, optionally with a timeout
\fB\-u\fP                    deprecated fixed\-size updates; see LIMITATIONS
.EE
.PP
Monitors and information:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-xs\fP \fIN\fP                 Xinerama screen number; requires Xinerama support
\fB\-output\fP \fINAME\fP          follow an XRandR output; requires XRandR support
\fB\-lm\fP                   list connected XRandR outputs with an active CRTC
\fB\-v\fP                    version and enabled features (requires X display)
.EE
.PP
Defaults: foreground grey70, background #111111, fixed font, title
centered, slave left\-aligned, no border or padding, no slave window, no
EOF persistence.
Line height defaults to font height + 2.
Without explicit widths, available target geometry is used.
See MONITOR SELECTION for target selection.
Options affecting the same value are processed in command\-line order.
.PP
\fB\-fn\-preload\fP
\fIFONT1,FONT2,\&...\fP loads up to 64
comma\-separated fonts before processing input.
Aliases are numbered from zero:
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fIdfnt0\fP\fB)\fP
and
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI0\fP\fB)\fP
select the first font,
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fIdfnt1\fP\fB)\fP
and
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI1\fP\fB)\fP
the second.
Spaces around font names are trimmed.
Use x: for X11 core fonts or xft: for XFT fonts; the latter requires an
XFT\-enabled build.
Preloading works with either backend and does not replace the normal
font selected by \fB\-fn\fP.
Use \fB\[ha]fn()\fP to restore it.
.PP
\fB\-b\fP
\fISPEC\fP adds an outer border.
SPEC accepts one, two, or four non\-negative widths and an optional X11
color:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-b\fP 10
\fB\-b\fP 10,8
\fB\-b\fP 10,8,10,8,red
.EE
.PP
One width applies to every side; two mean vertical,horizontal; four mean
top,right,bottom,left.
Without a color, the border follows
\fB\-bg\fP and later
\fB\[ha]normbg(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
changes.
Borders do not change explicit content sizes or clickable\-area
coordinates.
\fB\-b\fP
\fI0\fP disables the border.
.PP
\fB\[ha]border(\fP\fISPEC\fP\fB)\fP
uses the same grammar and replaces the border at runtime.
It must occupy its own input line.
Invalid runtime specifications are silently ignored.
.PP
\fB\-pad\fP
\fISPEC\fP adds padding around the union
of the visible title and slave content.
SPEC accepts one, two, or four non\-negative widths:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-pad\fP 10
\fB\-pad\fP 10,8
\fB\-pad\fP 10,8,10,8
.EE
.PP
One width applies to every side; two mean vertical,horizontal; four mean
top,right,bottom,left.
Padding always uses the normal \fB\-bg\fP
color, including later
\fB\[ha]normbg(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
changes.
\fB\[ha]padding(\fP\fISPEC\fP\fB)\fP
replaces all four sides at runtime, and
\fB\[ha]padding(\fP\fI0\fP\fB)\fP
disables it.
The command must occupy its own input line; invalid runtime
specifications are silently ignored.
.PP
The visible box model is \fBborder \-> padding \->
content\fP.
Explicit \fB\-x\fP,
\fB\-y\fP,
\fB\-w\fP,
\fB\-tw\fP, and
\fB\-h\fP values continue to describe
content; padding and border grow outward and are included in dock
struts.
A single padding box surrounds the visible title/slave union, so it does
not add a gap between them.
Padding does not change text or clickable\-area coordinates.
.PP
\fB\-underline\fP
\fITHICKNESS[,COLOR]\fP and
\fB\-overline\fP
\fITHICKNESS[,COLOR]\fP configure default
span\-decoration styles.
They do not enable decorations by themselves.
The built\-in default for each is one pixel using the normal foreground
color.
An omitted color follows \fB\-fg\fP and
later
\fB\[ha]normfg(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
changes; an explicit color is independent.
Specifications are strict and contain no whitespace:
\fB2,orange\fP is valid, while
\fB2, orange\fP is not.
.PP
Use
\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
and
\fB\[ha]overline(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
in input to enable a decoration.
An empty argument uses the configured defaults, a single argument may
override the thickness or color, and two arguments override both.
\fBoff\fP closes the active span:
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]overline()\fPtext and \fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI20x8\fP\fB)\fP graphics\fB\[ha]overline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP
\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fI2,lightblue\fP\fB)\fPunderlined\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP
.EE
.PP
Decorated spans include text, spaces, icons, rectangles, circles, block
alignment, and horizontal
\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP/\fB\[ha]pa(\fP\fI\&...\fP\fB)\fP
movement.
They use constant memory and normal painter order, so later explicitly
positioned content may overwrite a previously closed decoration.
Decorations are clipped inside the line and do not affect geometry or
clickable areas.
.SH MONITOR SELECTION
Without a monitor selector, dzen uses the root\-window geometry and
follows XRandR changes to its size.
.PP
\fB\-output\fP
\fINAME\fP selects a physical XRandR
output by name.
Dzen follows changes to that output\[cq]s mode, position and rotation.
If the output is disconnected, its windows and dock struts are hidden;
reconnecting it restores the windows with the original
\fB\-x\fP,
\fB\-y\fP,
\fB\-tw\fP and
\fB\-w\fP settings.
An unknown output name is an error.
Use \fB\-lm\fP to list connected outputs
which currently have an active CRTC.
.PP
\fB\-output\fP and
\fB\-xs\fP are mutually exclusive.
The legacy \fB\-xs\fP selector is static:
values from 1 through the number of Xinerama screens select that screen,
while 0, negative and out\-of\-range values use the root geometry.
Geometry is resolved once at startup and later XRandR events are ignored
when \fB\-xs\fP was explicitly supplied.
.SH MULTI\-LINE WINDOWS
Enables support for displaying multiple lines.
The parameter to \[lq]\fB\-l\fP\[rq]
specifies the number of lines to be displayed.
.PP
These lines of input are held in the slave window which becomes active
as soon as the pointer enters the title (default action) window.
.PP
If the mouse leaves the slave window it will be hidden unless it is set
sticky by clicking with Button2 into it (default action).
.PP
Button4 and Button5 (mouse wheel) will scroll the slave window up and
down if the content exceeds the window height (default action).
.SH MENUS
Dzen provides two menu modes, vertical and horizontal menus.
You can access these modes by adding `v'(vertical) or `h'(horizontal) to
the \[cq]\fB\-m\fP\[cq] option.
If nothing is specified dzen defaults to vertical menus.
.PP
Vertical menu, both invocations are equivalent:
.IP
.EX
\fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP \fB\-l\fP 4 \fB\-m\fP < file
\fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP \fB\-l\fP 4 \fB\-m\fP v < file
.EE
.PP
Horizontal menu:
.IP
.EX
\fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP \fB\-l\fP 4 \fB\-m\fP h < file
.EE
.PP
All actions beginning with \[lq]menu\[rq] work on the selected menu
entry.
.PP
Note: Menu mode only makes sense if
\fB\-l\fP
\fI\fP is specified!
Horizontal menus do not display title content.
Title\-only actions are otherwise ignored, but \f[CR]hide\f[R],
\f[CR]unhide\f[R], and \f[CR]togglehide\f[R] control the complete
horizontal surface.
.SH X RESOURCES
Startup precedence: built\-in defaults < X resources < command\-line
options.
As an example you can add following lines to \[ti]/.Xresources
.IP
.EX
\fBdzen2\fP.font:       \-*\-fixed\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*\-*
\fBdzen2\fP.foreground: green
\fBdzen2\fP.background: black
\fBdzen2\fP.underline:  2,orange
\fBdzen2\fP.overline:   1,lightblue
\fBdzen2\fP.titlename:  dzen title
\fBdzen2\fP.slavename:  dzen slave
.EE
.PP
Decoration resources use the same
\fBTHICKNESS[,COLOR]\fP grammar as their
command line options.
Command\-line values override X resources.
.SH FORMATTING COMMANDS
These commands may be mixed with text.
They change rendering, not global window geometry.
Colors accept X11 names or #rrggbb values.
.SS Colors:
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIcolor\fP\fB)\fP         Set foreground color
\fB\[ha]fg()\fP              Without arguments, sets default fg color
\fB\[ha]bg(\fP\fIcolor\fP\fB)\fP         Set background color
\fB\[ha]bg()\fP              Without arguments, sets default bg color

\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fIFONT\fP\fB)\fP          Select font for subsequent content in this line
\fB\[ha]fn()\fP              Restore the normal font
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fIdfnt0\fP\fB)\fP         Select the first font from \fB\-fn\-preload\fP
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI0\fP\fB)\fP             Numeric alias for the same preloaded font
                   Prefix xft: or x: to select the font backend.
                   XFT fonts require an XFT\-enabled build.
.EE
.SS Graphics:
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]i(\fP\fIpath\fP\fB)\fP           Draw icon specified by path
                   supported formats: XBM and optionally XPM

\fB\[ha]r(\fP\fIWIDTHxHEIGHT\fP\fB)\fP   Draw a rectangle with the dimensions
                   WIDTH and HEIGHT
\fB\[ha]ro(\fP\fIWIDTHxHEIGHT\fP\fB)\fP  Rectangle outline

\fB\[ha]c(\fP\fIDIAMETER\fP\fB)\fP         Draw a circle with diameter DIAMETER pixels
\fB\[ha]co(\fP\fIDIAMETER\fP\fB)\fP        Circle outline

\fB\[ha]underline()\fP       Enable underline with configured defaults
\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIARG\fP\fB)\fP    Enable underline with a thickness, color, or both
\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP    Disable underline
\fB\[ha]overline()\fP        Enable overline with configured defaults
\fB\[ha]overline(\fP\fIARG\fP\fB)\fP     Enable overline with a thickness, color, or both
\fB\[ha]overline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP     Disable overline
.EE
.SS Positioning:
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fIARGUMENT\fP\fB)\fP       Position next input amount of PIXELs to the right
                   or left of the current position
                   a.k.a. relative positioning

\fB\[ha]pa(\fP\fIARGUMENT\fP\fB)\fP      Position next input at PIXEL
                   a.k.a. absolute positioning
                   For maximum predictability \fB\[ha]pa()\fP should only be
                   used with \fB\-ta\fP \fIl\fP or \fB\-sa\fP \fIl\fP

 Where ARGUMENT:

 \fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI+\-X\fP\fB)\fP           Move X pixels to the right or left of the current position (on the X axis)

 \fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI+\-X;+\-Y\fP\fB)\fP       Move X pixels to the right or left and Y pixels up or down of the current
                   position (on the X and Y axis)

 \fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI;+\-Y\fP\fB)\fP          Move Y pixels up or down of the current position (on the Y axis)

 \fB\[ha]p()\fP              Without parameters resets the Y position to its default

 \fB\[ha]pa()\fP             Takes the same parameters as described above but positions at
                   the absolute X and Y coordinates

 Further \fB\[ha]p()\fP also takes some symbolic names as argument:

 _LOCK_X           Lock the current X position, useful if you want to
                   align things vertically
 _UNLOCK_X         Unlock the X position
 _LEFT             Move current x\-position to the left edge
 _RIGHT            Move current x\-position to the right edge
 _TOP              Move current y\-position to the top edge
 _CENTER           Move current x\-position to center of the window
 _BOTTOM           Move current y\-position to the bottom edge

\fB\[ha]ba(\fP\fIWIDTH,ALIGN\fP\fB)\fP   Align the next text run in WIDTH pixels
                   ALIGN is _LEFT, _CENTER or _RIGHT
\fB\[ha]ba()\fP              Cancel pending block alignment
                   Block alignment resets after drawing that text run.

\fB\[ha]left()\fP            Align next input to left. Reset settings (fg, bg, fn, etc)
\fB\[ha]center()\fP          Align next input to center. Reset settings (fg, bg, fn, etc)
\fB\[ha]right()\fP           Align next input to right. Reset settings (fg, bg, fn, etc)
                   Example:
                     \fB\[ha]left()\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIred\fP\fB)\fPLeft \fB\[ha]center()\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIgreen\fP\fB)\fPCenter \fB\[ha]right()\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIblue\fP\fB)\fPRight
.EE
.SS Interaction:
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]ca(\fP\fIBTN, CMD\fP\fB)\fP ... \fB\[ha]ca()\fP

                   Used to define \[aq]clickable areas\[aq] anywhere inside the
                   title window or slave window.
                   \- \[aq]BTN\[aq] denotes the mouse button (1=left, 2=middle, 3=right, etc.)
                   \- \[aq]CMD\[aq] denotes the command that should be spawned when the specific
                     area has been clicked with the defined button
                   \- \[aq]...\[aq] denotes any text or formatting commands dzen accepts
                   \- \[aq]\fB\[ha]ca()\fP\[aq] without arguments denotes the end of this clickable area

                   Example: \fB\[ha]ca(\fP\fI1,echo clicked\fP\fB)\fPClick me\fB\[ha]ca()\fP

\fB\[ha]ib(\fP\fI1\fP\fB)\fP             Do not paint backgrounds over earlier content
\fB\[ha]ib(\fP\fI0\fP\fB)\fP             Resume background painting (the default)
.EE
.PP
Use
\fB\[ha]ib(\fP\fI1\fP\fB)\fP
with positioning commands to overlay shapes.
XBM icons use foreground/background colors; XPM requires an XPM\-enabled
build.
Paths are relative to the process working directory.
Run direct README.dzen demonstrations from the project directory so the
example bitmaps can be found.
The installed \fBdzen2\-help\fP sets its
working directory to the documentation directory for these assets.
.SS A compact status bar
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]left()\fP\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIgold\fP\fB)\fP 1 \fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP 2  3\fB\[ha]center()\fPEditor\fB\[ha]right()\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIlimegreen\fP\fB)\fPonline\fB\[ha]fg()\fP 12:34
.EE
.PP
Each alignment command starts a new region and resets its formatting.
The workspace number and time are static sample data; your script
supplies the current values.
.SS Status with foreground and background colors
.IP
.EX
Status: \fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIlimegreen\fP\fB)\fPonline\fB\[ha]fg()\fP | Errors: \fB\[ha]bg(\fP\fImaroon\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIwhite\fP\fB)\fP 3 \fB\[ha]fg()\fP\fB\[ha]bg()\fP | normal
.EE
.PP
The error count has white text on a maroon background.
Empty \fB\[ha]fg()\fP and
\fB\[ha]bg()\fP restore normal colors for
subsequent text.
.SS Spacing between fields
.IP
.EX
CPU\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI12\fP\fB)\fP24%\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI24\fP\fB)\fPRAM\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI12\fP\fB)\fP42%
.EE
.PP
\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fIN\fP\fB)\fP
moves the next element N pixels to the right of the current position.
.SS A clickable launcher
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]ca(\fP\fI1,xterm\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIlightblue\fP\fB)\fP[ Terminal ]\fB\[ha]fg()\fP\fB\[ha]ca()\fP  ordinary text
.EE
.PP
Left\-click the bracketed label to launch xterm (which must be
installed).
\fB\[ha]ca()\fP closes the clickable area;
the following text is outside it.
.SS Fixed\-width text columns
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]ba(\fP\fI90,_LEFT\fP\fB)\fPCPU\fB\[ha]ba(\fP\fI50,_RIGHT\fP\fB)\fP9%
\fB\[ha]ba(\fP\fI90,_LEFT\fP\fB)\fPMemory\fB\[ha]ba(\fP\fI50,_RIGHT\fP\fB)\fP42%
.EE
.PP
Each label occupies 90 pixels and each value occupies 50 pixels, aligned
right.
Block alignment applies to the next text run, so repeat it per field.
.SS A progress bar with a value
.IP
.EX
CPU \fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIgray30\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI100x8\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI\-100\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIlimegreen\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI35x8\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI65\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg()\fP 35%
.EE
.PP
Draw a 100\-pixel track, move back 100 pixels and draw a 35\-pixel fill.
Move forward the remaining 65 pixels before writing the value.
Your script calculates the fill width; dzen does not calculate
percentages.
.SS Underline and overline spans
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fI1,lightblue\fP\fB)\fPText and \fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI20x8\fP\fB)\fP graphics\fB\[ha]underline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP \fB\[ha]overline()\fPOverlined text\fB\[ha]overline(\fP\fIoff\fP\fB)\fP
.EE
.SS Positioned graphics
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]ib(\fP\fI1\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIred\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]ro(\fP\fI100x15\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI\-98\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIblue\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI20x10\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIorange\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI3\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]r(\fP\fI40x10\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI4\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIdarkgreen\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]co(\fP\fI12\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]p(\fP\fI2\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]c(\fP\fI10\fP\fB)\fP
.EE
.SS XBM icons
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]i(\fP\fIbitmaps/envelope.xbm\fP\fB)\fP Mail \fB\[ha]fg(\fP\fIseagreen\fP\fB)\fP\fB\[ha]i(\fP\fIbitmaps/battery.xbm\fP\fB)\fP Battery
.EE
.SS Literal carets
.IP
.EX
Two literal carets: \[ha]\[ha]\[ha]\[ha]
.EE
.SH CONTROL COMMANDS
Send each command as the first and only content of its input line.
Do not concatenate control commands or append display text to them.
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]togglecollapse()\fP
\fB\[ha]collapse()\fP
\fB\[ha]uncollapse()\fP
\fB\[ha]togglestick()\fP
\fB\[ha]stick()\fP            See EVENTS AND ACTIONS for a detailed description
\fB\[ha]unstick()\fP          of each command.
\fB\[ha]togglehide()\fP
\fB\[ha]hide()\fP
\fB\[ha]unhide()\fP
\fB\[ha]raise()\fP
\fB\[ha]lower()\fP
\fB\[ha]scrollhome()\fP
\fB\[ha]scrollend()\fP
\fB\[ha]exit()\fP

\fB\[ha]cs()\fP              Clear the slave buffer
\fB\[ha]normfg(\fP\fICOLOR\fP\fB)\fP     Change the normal foreground, restored by \fB\[ha]fg()\fP
\fB\[ha]normbg(\fP\fICOLOR\fP\fB)\fP     Change the normal background, restored by \fB\[ha]bg()\fP
\fB\[ha]normfn(\fP\fIFONT\fP\fB)\fP      Change the normal font, restored by \fB\[ha]fn()\fP
\fB\[ha]border(\fP\fISPEC\fP\fB)\fP      Replace the outer border; same syntax as \fB\-b\fP
\fB\[ha]padding(\fP\fISPEC\fP\fB)\fP     Replace padding; same syntax as \fB\-pad\fP
.EE
.PP
Invalid border/padding specifications are ignored.
These commands replace the complete specification, not selected sides.
Other command arguments follow their own parsers; there is no universal
whitespace\-trimming rule.
.PP
Routing command (unlike the above, it carries display text):
.IP
.EX
\fB\[ha]tw()\fPTEXT          Draw TEXT to title; put the command first, once per line
.EE
.SH EVENTS AND ACTIONS
Dzen allows the user to associate actions to events.
.PP
The command line syntax is as follows:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-e\fP \[aq]event1=action1:option1:...option<n>,...,action<m>;...;event<l>\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Every event can take any number of actions and every action can take any
number of options.
(By default limited to 64 each, easily changeable in action.h)
.PP
An example:
.IP
.EX
\fB\-e\fP \[aq]button1=exec:xterm:firefox;entertitle=uncollapse,unhide;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Meaning:
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBbutton1=exec:xterm:firefox;\fP On
Button1 event (Button1 release on the mouse) execute xterm and firefox.
.PP
Note: xterm and firefox are options to the exec action
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBentertitle=uncollapse,unhide;\fP On
entertitle (mouse pointer enters the title window) uncollapse slave
window and unhide the title window
.IP \[bu] 2
\fBbutton3=exit\fP On button3 event exit
dzen
.SS Supported events:
.IP
.EX
\fBonstart\fP             Perform actions right after startup
\fBonexit\fP              Perform actions just before exiting
\fBonnewinput\fP          Perform actions if there is new input for the slave window
\fBbutton1\fP             Mouse button1 released
\fBbutton2\fP             Mouse button2 released
\fBbutton3\fP             Mouse button3 released
\fBbutton4\fP             Mouse button4 released (usually scrollwheel)
\fBbutton5\fP             Mouse button5 released (usually scrollwheel)
\fBbutton6\fP             Mouse button6 released
\fBbutton7\fP             Mouse button7 released
\fBentertitle\fP          Mouse enters the title window
\fBleavetitle\fP          Mouse leaves the title window
\fBenterslave\fP          Mouse enters the slave window
\fBleaveslave\fP          Mouse leaves the slave window
\fBsigusr1\fP             SIGUSR1 received
\fBsigusr2\fP             SIGUSR2 received
\fBkey_KEYNAME\fP         Keyboard events (*)

(*) Keyboard events:
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-

Every key can be bound to an action (see below). The format is:
\[ga]key_KEYNAME\[ga] where KEYNAME is the name of the key as defined in
keysymdef.h (usually: /usr/include/X11/keysymdef.h).  The part
after \[ga]XK_\[ga] in keysymdef.h must be used for KEYNAME.
.EE
.SS Supported actions:
.IP
.EX
\fBexec\fP:command1:..:n  execute all given options
\fBmenuexec\fP            executes selected menu entry
\fBexit\fP:retval         exit dzen and return \[aq]retval\[aq]
\fBprint\fP:str1:...:n    write all given options to STDOUT
\fBmenuprint\fP           write selected menu entry to STDOUT after parsing formatting
\fBmenuprint_noparse\fP   write selected menu entry to STDOUT with formatting commands intact
\fBcollapse\fP            collapse (roll\-up) slave window
\fBuncollapse\fP          uncollapse (roll\-down) slave window
\fBtogglecollapse\fP      toggle collapsed state
\fBstick\fP               stick slave window
\fBunstick\fP             unstick slave window
\fBtogglestick\fP         toggle sticky state
\fBhide\fP                strictly hide the title surface
\fBunhide\fP              restore the hidden surface
\fBtogglehide\fP          toggle strict hide state
\fBraise\fP               raise window to view (above all others)
\fBlower\fP               lower window (behind all others)
\fBscrollhome\fP          show head of input
\fBscrollend\fP           show tail of input
\fBscrollup\fP:n          scroll slave window n lines up   (default n=1)
\fBscrolldown\fP:n        scroll slave window n lines down (default n=1)
\fBgrabkeys\fP            enable keyboard support
\fBungrabkeys\fP          disable keyboard support
\fBgrabmouse\fP           enable mouse support
                    only needed with specific windowmanagers, such as fluxbox
\fBungrabmouse\fP         release mouse
                    only needed with specific windowmanagers, such as fluxbox
.EE
.PP
\fBhide\fP fully unmaps title\-only,
collapsed vertical, and horizontal surfaces.
An expanded vertical menu keeps its slave visible.
\fBunhide\fP restores the previous
collapsed or expanded state.
A fully unmapped surface cannot receive pointer events; restore it with
a signal or an active key grab.
.IP
.EX
Note:   If no events/actions are specified dzen defaults to:

    Title only mode:
    \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-

    \fB\-e\fP \[aq]button3=exit:13\[aq]


    Multiple lines and vertical menu mode:
    \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-

    \fB\-e\fP \[aq]entertitle=uncollapse,grabkeys;
        enterslave=grabkeys;leaveslave=collapse,ungrabkeys;
        button1=menuexec;button2=togglestick;button3=exit:13;
        button4=scrollup;button5=scrolldown;
        key_Escape=ungrabkeys,exit\[aq]


    Horizontal menu mode:
    \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-

    \fB\-e\fP \[aq]enterslave=grabkeys;leaveslave=ungrabkeys;
        button4=scrollup;button5=scrolldown;
        key_Left=scrollup;key_Right=scrolldown;
        button1=menuexec;button3=exit:13;
        key_Escape=ungrabkeys,exit\[aq]


    If you define any events/actions, there is no default behaviour,
    i.e. you will have to specify _all_ events/actions you want to
    use.
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
Update a title without appending to the slave:
.IP
.EX
{
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Status\[aq] \[aq]Service running\[aq] \[aq]Connected\[aq]
    sleep 2
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]tw()\fPStatus: updated\[aq]
} | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-p\fP \fB\-e\fP \[aq]onstart=uncollapse;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Replace the slave contents and update the title:
.IP
.EX
{
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Tasks\[aq] \[aq]Build: waiting\[aq] \[aq]Tests: waiting\[aq]
    sleep 2
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]cs()\fP\[aq] \[aq]Build: done\[aq] \[aq]Tests: passed\[aq]
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]tw()\fPTasks: complete\[aq]
} | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-p\fP \fB\-e\fP \[aq]onstart=uncollapse;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.PP
\fB\[ha]cs()\fP clears the old slave buffer
without replacing the title.
The next two lines refill the slave;
\fB\[ha]tw()\fP explicitly replaces the
title.
Each control command is sent on its own line, not mixed into a rendered
line.
.PP
Change window styling at runtime; commands occupy separate lines:
.IP
.EX
{
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Initial theme\[aq]
    sleep 2
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]normbg(\fP\fIdarkgreen\fP\fB)\fP\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]padding(\fP\fI4,8\fP\fB)\fP\[aq]
    printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Updated theme (bg changed, padding added)\[aq]
} | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-p\fP \fB\-b\fP \[aq]1,seagreen\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Return the selected menu entry to stdout:
.IP
.EX
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Choose\[aq] \[aq]First\[aq] \[aq]Second\[aq] |
    \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-m\fP v \fB\-p\fP \[rs]
      \fB\-e\fP \[aq]onstart=uncollapse;button1=menuprint,exit;button3=exit\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Launch an application from a horizontal menu:
.IP
.EX
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Applications\[aq] \[aq]xterm\[aq] \[aq]xclock\[aq] |
    \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 2 \fB\-m\fP h \fB\-p\fP
.EE
.PP
The first line is still supplied, although horizontal menus do not show
title content.
The default button1 binding executes the selected entry.
.PP
Show a growing log:
.IP
.EX
{ printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]Log\[aq]; tail \-f application.log; } |
    \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 12 \fB\-w\fP 600 \fB\-p\fP
.EE
.PP
Provide your own application.log.
Hover over the title to expand the log; use the wheel to scroll.
Menu execution and clickable commands execute shell commands, so only
use trusted command strings.
.PP
Preload fonts and switch between them within a multilingual greeting:
.IP
.EX
printf \[aq]%s\[rs]n\[aq] \[aq]\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI0\fP\fB)\fPHello, World! \fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI1\fP\fB)\fP今日は！ \fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI2\fP\fB)\fP😊🚀 \fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI3\fP\fB)\fP12345\[aq] |
    \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-fn\fP \[aq]xft:DejaVu Sans\[aq] \[rs]
        \fB\-fn\-preload\fP \[dq]xft:DejaVu Sans,\[rs]
            xft:Noto Sans CJK JP,\[rs]
            xft:Noto Color Emoji,\[rs]
            x:fixed\[dq] \[rs]
        \fB\-h\fP 32 \fB\-w\fP 600 \fB\-ta\fP l \fB\-p\fP 3
.EE
.PP
This example requires an XFT\-enabled build and the listed fonts
installed.
Preloaded fonts can be selected by name as well as by numbered alias.
The fourth font uses x: to select an X11 core font;
\fB\[ha]fn(\fP\fI3\fP\fB)\fP
renders 12345 with it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\fBdzen\fP uses two different approaches to
terminate itself:
.IP \[bu] 2
Timed termination: if EOF is received \-> terminate
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
unless the \fB\-p\fP option is set
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\-p\fP Without argument persist forever
.IP \[bu] 2
\fB\-p\fP With argument n persist for n
seconds, only when the mouse is not over the window.
.RE
.RE
.IP \[bu] 2
Interactive termination: if mouse button3 is clicked \-> terminate
.RS 2
.IP \[bu] 2
this is the default behaviour, see EVENTS AND ACTIONS
.IP \[bu] 2
in some modes the Escape key terminates too, see EVENTS AND ACTIONS
.RE
.PP
\fBSIGTERM\fP performs the normal shutdown
lifecycle, including the \fBonexit\fP
event, and exits with status 143.
An \fBexit:N\fP action attached to
\fBonexit\fP does not override that status.
A \fB\-p\fP
\fIN\fP timeout also performs
\fBonexit\fP, but exits with status 0.
.PP
Return values:
.IP
.EX
0       Normal EOF or persistence timeout
1       An error; inspect stderr
143     SIGTERM, after onexit
N       An explicit exit:N action (default right\-click uses 13)
.EE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Input lines are limited to 16 MiB; excess bytes are discarded until the
next newline.
An unterminated final fragment is not a complete input message.
Individual command arguments and clickable areas have separate limits.
This is an X11 application; it needs an X server, including for
\fB\-v\fP.
.PP
Use printf instead of shell\-dependent echo
\fB\-e\fP or print in portable examples.
Menu execution uses the entry as a command; menuprint writes it to
stdout.
.PP
Troubleshooting:
.IP \[bu] 2
Window closes immediately: stdin reached EOF; use
\fB\-p\fP.
.IP \[bu] 2
Slave is invisible: \fB\-l\fP enables it
but default bindings wait for a hover.
With custom \fB\-e\fP, include
onstart=uncollapse or an appropriate pointer event.
.IP \[bu] 2
Right\-click stopped closing: your
\fB\-e\fP replaced the defaults; add
button3=exit.
.IP \[bu] 2
Hidden window cannot be hovered: restore through sigusr1=unhide and
SIGUSR1, or a previously active keyboard grab.
.IP \[bu] 2
Misplaced absolute content: use \fB\-ta\fP
l / \fB\-sa\fP l with
\fB\[ha]pa()\fP.
.IP \[bu] 2
Feature option is unavailable: check configure features and
\fBdzen2\fP
\fB\-v\fP.
.PP
Deprecated \fB\-u\fP (simultaneous
fixed\-size batches):
.PP
This option provides facilities to update the title and slave window at
the same time.
.PP
The way it works is best described by an example:
.IP
.EX
Motivation:

We want to display an updating clock in the title and some log
output in the slave window.

Solution:

while true; do
      date                # output goes to the title window
      dmesg | tail \-n 10  # output goes to the slave window
      sleep 1
done | \fBdzen2\fP \fB\-l\fP 10 \fB\-u\fP
.EE
.PP
For this to work correctly it is essential to provide exactly the number
of lines to the slave window as defined by the parameter to
\fB\-l\fP.
.PP
Prefer explicit \fB\[ha]tw()\fP updates in
new scripts.
.SH INSTALLATION
Build dependencies: a C toolchain, GNU Autotools and Xlib headers.
Optional features require their corresponding development libraries.
.IP
.EX
autoreconf \-vfi
\&./configure \-\-enable\-gadgets \-\-enable\-xft \-\-enable\-xpm \[rs]
    \-\-enable\-xinerama \-\-enable\-xrandr \-\-enable\-xcursor
make
make install
.EE
.PP
Use \[en]disable\-FEATURE to omit an optional feature.
See each gadget\[cq]s README for its interface.
.SH DEVELOPMENT
.SS Build and change code
Configure the features you need as described in INSTALLATION, then use:
.IP
.EX
make          # build dzen2 and update documentation and changed screenshots
make check    # run tests, including documentation checks
.EE
.PP
After changing configure.ac or Makefile.am, rerun autoreconf \-vfi and
configure.
Run make format after editing C sources or headers; it requires
clang\-format.
.PP
Source layout:
.IP \[bu] 2
src/main.c handles startup, options and the event loop.
.IP \[bu] 2
src/draw.c parses inline rendering and whole\-line control commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
src/action.c implements event actions; src/action.h declares them.
.IP \[bu] 2
src/font.c handles fonts; src/caches.c manages rendering caches.
.IP \[bu] 2
gadgets/ contains the auxiliary programs.
.IP \[bu] 2
build\-aux/ contains documentation and build helpers.
.PP
Keep inline drawing commands separate from whole\-line control commands.
For new settings, preserve the order: built\-in defaults, X resources,
then command\-line overrides.
Add user\-visible changes to the documentation and include new
source/test files in the appropriate Makefile.am.
.SS Testing changes
make check runs the tests applicable to the configured build.
Unit tests live in tests/unit/; integration tests under
tests/integration/ cover signals, rendering and monitor changes.
Use CHECK from test_common.h in C tests so checks also run when
assertions are disabled.
.PP
For a focused rendering check, run the visual runner with a case file
and optionally the line number of an individual case:
.IP
.EX
tests/integration/visual/runner.sh tests/integration/visual/xft/cases.md
tests/integration/visual/runner.sh tests/integration/visual/xft/cases.md:286
.EE
.PP
The runner uses an isolated Xvfb display.
Review actual images and diffs before explicitly updating expected
images for an intentional rendering change.
Keep temporary actual/diff files out of commits.
Set NO_COLOR=1 for plain test output.
Use make distcheck when changing build or installation rules; it also
checks building, installing and cleaning a distribution archive.
.SS Editing documentation
Edit README.dzen and run the same make command used for code changes.
README.md and dzen2.1 are generated files.
New or changed examples and missing PNGs are rendered automatically into
docs/screenshots/; prose\-only changes do not take screenshots.
Commit generated documentation, PNGs, text caches and manifest together
with README.dzen.
Delete a PNG to regenerate it after changing fonts or rendering code
without changing the example text.
.PP
Source format:
.IP \[bu] 2
Use
\fB\[ha]foo(\fP\fIbar\fP\fB)\fP
for displayed dzen commands and \[ha]foo(bar) for live commands.
.IP \[bu] 2
Use \fB\-foo\fP
\fIbar\fP for CLI references and code
spans for event/action names.
.IP \[bu] 2
Use ordinary headings; dzen2\-help styles headings and references
automatically.
.IP \[bu] 2
Example blocks have a unique lowercase identifier (words/digits
separated by hyphens), followed by Input and Result blocks with
four\-space indentation and a blank line after each block.
Input doubles every caret and must decode exactly to Result.
Result contains static rendering commands only.
.PP
Do not add manual styling to descriptions or Input blocks.
Code blocks lose backticks around command references but preserve shell
substitutions.
Direct display of README.dzen remains possible without automatic
reference styling.
.PP
Text generation requires Bash, POSIX awk and Pandoc.
New screenshots also need Xvfb, xset, xdotool, xwd and ImageMagick.
Captures use the help viewer styling and the default font on a fresh X
server; font availability affects appearance.
.PP
See build\-aux/generate\-docs.awk for the generation pipeline and
focused targets.
